{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To determine the number of units in the set \\( S \\) of complex numbers of the form \\( a + bi \\) where \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are integers, we need to find all complex numbers \\( z = a + bi \\) such that there exists a \\( w = c + di \\) in \\( S \\) with \\( zw = 1 \\). This means that the product \\( (a + bi)(c + di) = 1 \\).\n\nFirst, we expand the product:\n\\[\n(a + bi)(c + di) = ac + adi + bci + bdi^2 = ac + adi + bci - bd = (ac - bd) + (ad + bc)i.\n\\]\nFor this product to equal 1, the real part must be 1 and the imaginary part must be 0. Therefore, we have the system of equations:\n\\[\nac - bd = 1,\n\\]\n\\[\nad + bc = 0.\n\\]\nFrom the second equation, we can solve for \\( d \\):\n\\[\nad + bc = 0 \\implies d = -\\frac{bc}{a} \\quad \\text{(assuming \\( a \\neq 0 \\))}.\n\\]\nSubstituting \\( d = -\\frac{bc}{a} \\) into the first equation, we get:\n\\[\nac - b\\left(-\\frac{bc}{a}\\right) = 1 \\implies ac + \\frac{b^2c}{a} = 1 \\implies \\frac{a^2c + b^2c}{a} = 1 \\implies c(a^2 + b^2) = a.\n\\]\nThus, \\( c = \\frac{a}{a^2 + b^2} \\). For \\( c \\) to be an integer, \\( a^2 + b^2 \\) must divide \\( a \\). Since \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are integers, the only way \\( a^2 + b^2 \\) can divide \\( a \\) is if \\( a = 0 \\) or \\( a^2 + b^2 = 1 \\).\n\n","prefix_end":1193,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,8253,279,1372,315,8153,304,279,738,17767,328,1124,8,315,6351,5109,315,279,1352,17767,264,488,6032,1124,8,1380,17767,264,1124,8,323,17767,293,1124,8,525,25780,11,582,1184,311,1477,678,6351,5109,17767,1147,284,264,488,6032,1124,8,1741,429,1052,6724,264,17767,289,284,272,488,1853,1124,8,304,17767,328,1124,8,448,17767,24396,284,220,16,1124,568,1096,3363,429,279,1985,17767,320,64,488,6032,2376,66,488,1853,8,284,220,16,1124,3593,5338,11,582,9225,279,1985,510,59,9640,2877,488,6032,2376,66,488,1853,8,